VALUES (
  '2026/05/13/hdq-thread-may-13-2026',
  'thread', 'thread',
  'The TSX Is Outperforming a Stagflation Print, and the Explanation Is the Same as the Risk',
  'Wednesday''s US PPI came in at +6% year over year, the hottest since 2022, pricing out any Fed cut and pushing hike probability to 39%. The TSX should be falling harder. It isn''t. The reason why is also the reason to be cautious.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>US PPI: +1.4% m/m, +6% y/y in April,</strong><span> the largest annual jump since April 2022, with goods up 2.0% and services up 1.2% in a single month. Fed hike probability by year-end climbed to 39% following the release.</span></li>
<li><strong>The TSX''s energy overweight is absorbing the shock.</strong><span> WTI settled at US$102.18 on Tuesday; Canadian Natural Resources gained 4.1% and Suncor 2.3% on the session. Energy is 18.1% of the TSX composite, versus 3.9% of the S&amp;P 500.</span></li>
<li><strong>Shopify continued its post-earnings slide,</strong><span> falling 2.43% on May 12 and trading near US$100, down from US$127.55 before its May 5 earnings release. As the third-largest TSX constituent by weight, its drag is structural, not incidental.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Trump-Xi Beijing summit is producing a proposed "Board of Trade" mechanism,</strong><span> targeting approximately US$30 billion in bilateral tariff reductions on non-sensitive goods. A deal removes one layer of Canadian stagflation risk but does not touch the Iran-driven oil shock.</span></li>
<li><strong>Bank of Canada June 10 decision: markets price 5% hike probability today,</strong><span> rising to 24% by the July 15 announcement. Canada''s April CPI, due May 19, is the next critical input into that calculus.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The US Producer Price Index for April came in at +1.4% month over month and +6.0% year over year on Wednesday, the largest annual print since April 2022, with goods prices jumping 2.0% in a single month. The Bureau of Labor Statistics attributed much of the goods acceleration to energy-related costs flowing from the Iran war and the Strait of Hormuz closure. Federal Reserve funds futures, which had already priced out all 2026 cuts following Tuesdays hot CPI reading, moved further: market-implied probability of a Fed hike by year-end reached 39% by midday, according to CME FedWatch data.</p><p>On a day this inflationary, the conventional expectation is a broad equity selloff, with rate-sensitive sectors and growth stocks leading the decline. That is exactly what happened in the United States, where the Nasdaq fell 0.71% on Tuesday and chip stocks that had surged on AI enthusiasm reversed sharply. Qualcomm fell 13% in Tuesdays session, its worst since 2020. Intel dropped 6.8%. The iShares Semiconductor ETF sank 5%. These are not marginal moves; they represent a repricing of the growth-at-any-cost trade that has driven US tech indices to records.</p><p>The TSX, by contrast, is not behaving like an index that just absorbed a six-percent annual inflation print.</p><h2>Why the TSX Is Partially Insulated and Why That Insulation Has a Ceiling</h2><p>The chart above shows the TSX composite sector weight distribution against the S&amp;P 500, as of April 30, 2026. The divergence between the two indices since the Iran war shock is legible in this data: where the S&amp;P 500 carries roughly 3.9% energy exposure and 31.2% technology exposure, the TSX carries 18.1% energy and 7.4% technology. An oil price shock that crushes the Nasdaq while lifting WTI above US$100 is, arithmetically, a relative tailwind for Canadian equities versus American ones.</p><div class="hdq-chart"><div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;"><div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;"><span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">TSX vs S&amp;P 500 -- SECTOR WEIGHTS</span><span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">Apr 30, 2026</span><span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">Index weights &nbsp;|&nbsp; % of index</span></div><div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 360" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;display:block;">
